.SH "NAME"
git-var \- Show a Git logical variable
.SH "SYNOPSIS"
.sp
.nf
\fBgit\fR \fBvar\fR [\fB\-z\fR] \fB\-l\fR
\fBgit\fR \fBvar\fR [\fB\-z\fR] \fI<variable>\fR\&...\:
.fi
.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.sp
Prints Git logical variables\&. When a single variable is requested, its bare value is printed, and the command exits with code 1 if it has no value\&. When multiple variables are requested, they are printed as \fBVARIABLE=value\fR pairs, separated by newlines; a variable that has no value is omitted from the output, which is not by itself an error\&.
.sp
If \fB\-z\fR is given, the output format changes depending on the mode:
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
With a single variable, the bare value is terminated by a NUL byte\&.
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
With multiple variables or with
\fB\-l\fR, the variable name and value are separated by a newline, and each entry is terminated by a NUL byte (\fBVARIABLE\envalue\e0\fR), in the same way as
\fBgit\fR
\fBconfig\fR
\fBlist\fR
\fB\-z\fR\&.
.RE
.SH "OPTIONS"
.PP
\fB\-l\fR
.RS 4
Display the logical variables\&. In addition, all the variables of the Git configuration file \&.git/config are listed as well\&. (However, the configuration variables listing functionality is deprecated in favor of
\fBgit\fR
\fBconfig\fR
\fBlist\fR\&.)
.RE
.PP
\fB\-z\fR
.RS 4
Terminate entries with NUL instead of newline\&. When used with
\fB\-l\fR
or when multiple variables are requested, the variable name and its value are separated by a newline, and each entry is terminated with a NUL byte\&.
.RE
.SH "EXAMPLES"
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Get the author identity:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
$ git var GIT_AUTHOR_IDENT
Eric W\&. Biederman <ebiederm@lnxi\&.com> 1121223278 \-0600
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.RE
.sp
.RS 4
.ie n \{\
\h'-04'\(bu\h'+03'\c
.\}
.el \{\
.sp -1
.IP \(bu 2.3
.\}
Get the author name and email:
.sp
.if n \{\
.RS 4
.\}
.nf
$ git var GIT_AUTHOR_NAME GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME=Eric W\&. Biederman
GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL=ebiederm@lnxi\&.com
.fi
.if n \{\
.RE
.\}
.RE
.SH "VARIABLES"
.PP
\fBGIT_AUTHOR_IDENT\fR, \fBGIT_AUTHOR_NAME\fR, \fBGIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL\fR, \fBGIT_AUTHOR_DATE\fR
.RS 4
The authorship information that would be recorded in the resulting commit object if you ran
\fBgit\fR
\fBcommit\fR
right now\&.
\fBGIT_AUTHOR_IDENT\fR
consists of the author\(cqs name, e\-mail address, and timestamp+timezone\&. These three pieces of information are available separately as
\fBGIT_AUTHOR_NAME\fR,
\fBGIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL\fR, and
\fBGIT_AUTHOR_DATE\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBGIT_COMMITTER_IDENT\fR, \fBGIT_COMMITTER_NAME\fR, \fBGIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL\fR, \fBGIT_COMMITTER_DATE\fR
.RS 4
The committer information that would be recorded in the resulting commit object if you ran
\fBgit\fR
\fBcommit\fR
right now\&.
\fBGIT_COMMITTER_IDENT\fR
consists of the committer\(cqs name, e\-mail address, and timestamp+timezone\&. These three pieces of information are available separately as
\fBGIT_COMMITTER_NAME\fR,
\fBGIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL\fR, and
\fBGIT_COMMITTER_DATE\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBGIT_EDITOR\fR
.RS 4
Text editor for use by Git commands\&. The value is meant to be interpreted by the shell when it is used\&. Examples:
\fB~/bin/vi\fR,
\fB$SOME_ENVIRONMENT_VARIABLE\fR, "C:\eProgram
\fBFiles\eVim\egvim\&.exe\fR"
\fB\-\-nofork\fR\&. The order of preference is
\fB$GIT_EDITOR\fR, then
\fBcore\&.editor\fR
configuration value, then
\fB$VISUAL\fR, then
\fB$EDITOR\fR, and then the default chosen at compile time, which is usually
\fIvi\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBGIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR\fR
.RS 4
Text editor used to edit the
\fItodo\fR
file while running
\fBgit\fR
\fBrebase\fR
\fB\-i\fR\&. Like
\fBGIT_EDITOR\fR, the value is meant to be interpreted by the shell when it is used\&. The order of preference is
\fB$GIT_SEQUENCE_EDITOR\fR, then
\fBsequence\&.editor\fR
configuration value, and then the value of
\fBgit\fR
\fBvar\fR
\fBGIT_EDITOR\fR\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBGIT_PAGER\fR
.RS 4
Text viewer for use by Git commands (e\&.g\&.,
\fIless\fR)\&. The value is meant to be interpreted by the shell\&. The order of preference is
\fB$GIT_PAGER\fR, then the value of
\fBcore\&.pager\fR
configuration, then
\fB$PAGER\fR, and then the default chosen at compile time (usually
\fBless\fR)\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBGIT_DEFAULT_BRANCH\fR
.RS 4
The name of the first branch created in newly initialized repositories\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBGIT_SHELL_PATH\fR
.RS 4
The path of the binary providing the POSIX shell for commands which use the shell\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBGIT_ATTR_SYSTEM\fR
.RS 4
The path to the system
\fBgitattributes\fR(5)
file, if one is enabled\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBGIT_ATTR_GLOBAL\fR
.RS 4
The path to the global (per\-user)
\fBgitattributes\fR(5)
file\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBGIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM\fR
.RS 4
The path to the system configuration file, if one is enabled\&.
.RE
.PP
\fBGIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL\fR
.RS 4
The path to the global (per\-user) configuration files, if any\&. This variable can have multiple values\&.
.RE
.sp
Most path values contain only one value\&. However, some can contain multiple values, which are separated by newlines (or NUL bytes if \fB\-z\fR is given), and are listed in order from highest to lowest priority\&. When querying multiple variables (or using \fB\-l\fR), each value is output as a separate \fBVARIABLE=value\fR entry (or \fBVARIABLE\envalue\e0\fR with \fB\-z\fR)\&. Callers should be prepared for any such path value to contain multiple items\&.
.sp
Note that paths are printed even if they do not exist, but not if they are disabled by other environment variables\&.
